Both 80-net-name-slot.rules and 80-net-setup-link.rules are symlinks to
/dev/null, which is the canonical way to mask one of udev's own rule files.

They disable PREDICTABLE INTERFACE NAMING. Without it the device manager renames
eth0 to enp2s0 on the HC-800, and every board.env in the tree names eth0 —
OHC_ID_IFACE, OHC_MDNS_IFACE, OHC_UPLINK_IFACE — so S40net finds nothing and the
box boots perfectly with no network. It looks exactly like a hang and is not.

TWO FILENAMES, because the rule lives in a different file depending on who
ships it:

    eudev          /lib/udev/rules.d/80-net-name-slot.rules   <- what we run
    systemd-udev   /lib/udev/rules.d/80-net-setup-link.rules

Masking only the systemd name did nothing here, and cost a full build cycle to
find. Masking a file that does not exist is harmless, so mask both and stop
caring which device manager the image happens to use.

The rule also honours a kernel command line flag — its first line is
`ENV{net.ifnames}=="0", GOTO="net_name_slot_end"` — but that would have to be
set in GRUB, the kexec cmdline, U-Boot and CEFDK separately, on five boards.
One file in the overlay covers all of them.

Masking rather than renaming the board.env keys, because the kernel name is
stable across this fleet (eth0 on x86, imx6 and davinci alike) while the
"predictable" name encodes the PCI path and would differ per board — the
opposite of what the rest of the naming convention is for.
